{#- A Connect that could not be read, said to the reader rather than to the client.

    The reason it is a page and not a redirect: the request that failed is one we could
    not verify, so the address it wanted to be sent back to is an address we have not
    checked. Handing an error to it would be following a stranger's redirect to say that
    we refused to trust the stranger.

    §6 governs the sentence: we own it, we say what they can do, and we do not blame the
    reader, who pressed a button in somebody else's app and has no idea what a redirect
    URI is. -#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>targum</title>
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}{{ asset('signin.css') }}</style>
{{ hebrew_face() }}
{{ chrome_face() }}
</head>
<body class="signin">
<main class="gate">
  <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="targum">
    <svg class="brand-mark" viewBox="20 12 56 72" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <rect x="20" y="12" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="currentColor"></rect>
      <rect x="54" y="22" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="var(--mark-accent)"></rect>
    </svg>
    <span>targum</span>
  </a>

  <div class="door">
    <h1>{{ t("connect.page.we-couldn-t-finish-that", "We couldn't finish that") }}</h1>
    <p class="said">{{ said }}</p>
    <p class="said">{{ t("connect.page.try-connecting-again-from-the-app", "Try connecting again from the app you started in.") }}</p>
    <p class="aside"><a href="/connect">{{ t("connect.page.how-connecting-works", "How connecting works") }}</a></p>
  </div>
</main>
</body>
</html>
